course: everyday
lesson: 121
title: Greeting adults and teachers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When a grown-up says hello to you, you look at them, smile and say hello back, using their name if you know it; ignoring a greeting feels rude to the other person.
explain: With adults it is safest to start formal: "Mr", "Mrs", "Ms" or "Dr" with their surname, until they tell you to use their first name.
explain: Every adult deserves the same kind greeting, whether they are the head teacher, the school caretaker, a cleaner or a dinner lady.
explain: Being polite never means you must talk to or follow a stranger: if an adult you do not know greets you when you are alone, you may say hello, but you do not stop, go anywhere or take anything, and you tell a trusted adult.
explain: A boy in Manchester meets his teacher in the supermarket on Saturday; he smiles and says "Hello, Mrs Patel", then lets her get on with her shopping.
fact: When an adult greets you, you look at them, smile and say hello back.
fact: Use "Mr", "Mrs", "Ms" or "Dr" with the surname until an adult asks you to use their first name.
fact: "Good morning" is said before midday, "good afternoon" until about six, and "good evening" after that.
fact: "Good night" is said when parting in the evening or going to bed, and the reply is "Good night" too.
fact: In a shop you greet the shopkeeper before asking for what you need.
fact: Children who greet adults themselves, instead of hiding behind a parent, seem confident and polite.
fact: Caretakers, cleaners and dinner ladies deserve the same respect as the head teacher.
fact: Take off your headphones before you answer someone who speaks to you.
fact: You may say hello to a stranger, but you never stop to chat, go anywhere or take anything, and you tell a trusted adult.
fact: A substitute or supply teacher deserves the same good behaviour as your usual teacher.
fact: When the head teacher walks into the classroom, pupils stop talking and look up, and some schools ask them to stand.
fact: Calling a teacher "Mum" or "Dad" by accident happens to almost everyone, so you laugh it off and carry on.
exercise: A grown-up friend of your parents says hello to you. What do you do? | Look at them, smile and say hello back | Look away and wait for your parent to answer | Nod without looking up from your game | Run off to play before they ask anything
exercise: You do not know whether to call an adult by first name. What is safest? | Use "Mr" or "Mrs" with the surname until they say otherwise | Use their first name every time, because it sounds much friendlier | Call them nothing and just start talking | Ask other children what nickname they use
exercise: You walk into a shop and the shopkeeper says "Good morning". What do you do first? | Say "Good morning" back, then ask for what you need | Ask for what you need straight away | Wait silently until the shopkeeper speaks to you a second time | Point at the thing you want without a word
exercise: You meet a grown-up you know while you are with your mum. What is polite? | Say hello to them yourself | Hide behind your mum until they leave | Let your mum say hello for you | Whisper to your mum to say you are busy
exercise: You see the school caretaker in the corridor. How should you greet him? | Just as kindly as you would greet the head teacher | Only if he speaks to you first | Less politely than a teacher, because he is not a teacher | Not at all, because he is busy working
exercise: An adult you do not know says hello when you are alone in the park. What is right? | Say hello if you like, but do not chat, go anywhere or take anything | You must stop and talk for a while, because ignoring any adult is rude | You should go with them if they ask nicely | You should accept a sweet so as not to be rude
exercise: You are wearing headphones when an adult speaks to you. What do you do? | Take them off before you answer | Turn the music down a little and nod | Answer loudly over the music | Keep them on and read their lips
exercise: Someone says "Good night" to you in the evening. What is the polite reply? | "Good night" | "Good morning" | "Good afternoon" | "Hello"
exercise: You meet a neighbour at four o'clock in the afternoon. What do you say? | "Good afternoon" | "Good morning" | "Good evening" | "Good night"
exercise: You arrive at class and the teacher is at the door. What do you say? | "Good morning, Mr Smith" | "Hey you, what's up?" | Nothing, you just walk in | "Good night, Mr Smith"
exercise: You meet your teacher in the supermarket at the weekend. What is best? | Smile, say hello, then let them get on with their shopping | Pretend you have not seen them | Follow them around the shop to chat | Ask them loudly about your homework marks in front of everyone
exercise: A substitute teacher comes to your class. How should the class behave? | As well as with the usual teacher | A bit worse, since she does not know the rules | Not greet her, since she is only there for a day | Tell her the usual teacher lets them play all day
exercise: You accidentally call your teacher "Mum". What should you do? | Laugh it off and carry on | Run out of the classroom in shame | Refuse to speak for the rest of the day | Pretend you said something else
exercise: The head teacher walks into your classroom. What do pupils usually do? | Stop talking and look up, and in some schools stand | Carry on chatting until she speaks | Hide their work under the desk so that she cannot see it | Clap and cheer loudly
exercise: Why is it never wrong to start with "Mr" or "Mrs"? | Adults like to choose how they are addressed, and formal is always polite | Because it is against the law for children to use a grown-up's own first name | Because adults do not like their first names | Because surnames are easier to remember
test: A girl in Texas is shy, and when her dad's friend greets her she stares at her shoes. What would be better? | Look up, smile and say "Hello" back | Keep staring, because shy children need not answer | Walk away so she does not feel awkward | Let her dad say she is rude
test: Which greeting fits seven o'clock in the evening? | "Good evening" | "Good morning" | "Good afternoon" | "Good night, see you at lunch"
test: Why do we greet the school cleaner as kindly as the principal, the head teacher? | Every worker deserves the same respect, whatever the job | Because the cleaner can give us detentions | Because the principal does not like greetings | Because cleaners are always older than principals and need more care
test: A woman you have never met says hello and asks you to help find her puppy. What do you do? | Do not go with her, and tell a trusted adult | Go with her, because she was polite | Help her for a few minutes and then come back | Ask her name and then go with her
test: Your neighbour, Dr Brown, says "Please call me Helen." What do you call her now? | Helen | Dr Brown, always, whatever she says | Mrs Helen Brown | Doctor
test: Why should you take out your earbuds when an adult talks to you? | It shows you are really listening to them | Because earbuds stop working near adults | Because adults want to borrow them | Because it is against school rules everywhere
test: A boy in Dublin hears a classmate call the teacher "Dad" and everyone giggles. What is the kind view? | It happens to almost everyone and teachers are used to it | He should be punished for being rude | He must stand up and apologise to the whole school in assembly | He should never speak in class again
test: Which is the most polite way to start in a shop? | Greet the shopkeeper, then ask for what you need | Ask for the price before saying anything else | Wait for the shopkeeper to guess what you want | Say "Oi!" to get the shopkeeper's attention
test: A new supply teacher looks nervous on her first morning. What helps most? | Greet her politely and behave well | Test her by breaking the rules | Ignore her until the usual teacher returns | Tell her the class does no work on Fridays
test: its 7 at night, what do i say to nan's friend when she comes in | Good evening | Good morning | Good afternoon | Good night
test: new teacher says call me mr patel, can i just say raj | No, say Mr Patel until he asks you to use his first name | Yes, first names are friendlier and teachers secretly like them more | Yes, if you say it with a smile | Yes, teachers prefer nicknames
test: lol i called my teacher mum in front of everyone | Laugh it off and carry on, it happens to almost everyone | Run out of the room | Pretend you didn't say it all year | Apologise every day for a week
test: man at the bus stop says hi and wants to chat | Say hello but don't chat or go anywhere; tell a trusted adult | Chat for a while to be polite, he might feel sad if you walk off | Go with him if he's friendly | Take a sweet if he offers
explain: A boy in Ohio was so keen to be polite that he answered every question from a friendly man at the park, including his address; the man turned up near his house, and his parents had to call the police.
explain: A girl in Leeds would not greet or answer her new teacher for weeks because she was shy; the teacher thought she was unhappy at school, and it took a long talk with her mum to sort it out.
fact: Being polite to adults never means you must give personal details, go anywhere with them or keep their secrets.
fact: Children who never greet or answer adults are often misunderstood as rude or upset, even when they are only shy.
test: nice man at the park asking where i live, be polite n tell him? | No, you can be polite and still not say, tell your adult | Yes, it's rude not to answer a grown-up who asks you nicely | Yes, if he smiles | Only tell him the street
test: too shy to say hi to my teacher, ever | Try a small hello or a wave, she'll understand you better | Never say anything to her | Hide behind your bag every morning so that she never sees you | Tell her to go away
test: grown-up i dont know says hop in the car, ur mum sent me | Say no, get away and tell a trusted adult | Get in, he knows your mum | Ask him a password then get in | Walk next to the car
test: did u hear Noah called the teacher "mum" in assembly n "the whole school laughed for a week". what's probs exaggerated | A whole week; it happens to almost everyone, so laugh it off | That calling a teacher mum by mistake is a slip that anyone makes | That some kids laughed at the time | That he carried on with his day after
test: my cousin's mate told a nice man at the park his address n the police came. real lesson? | Be polite but never give strangers personal details | Never say hello to any adult you do not know | It is fine to tell nice, friendly men your address if they smile | Only tell strangers your address if they ask twice
test: everyone says the new girl is "stuck up" cos she doesn't say hi to teachers. spread it? | No, she may just be shy, and it is unkind | Yes, everyone should know she is rude | Yes, but only tell the teachers about it | Yes, if you say you are only joking
exam: State the form of address appropriate for an adult until such time as that adult requests the use of a first name. | Mr, Mrs, Ms or Dr with the surname | The adult's first name used alone | The word "mate" or "buddy" alone | The adult's surname used alone
exam: Ascertain the appropriate greeting at a quarter past seven in the evening upon meeting a neighbour. | Good evening | Good morning | Good afternoon | Good night
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the greeting of an unknown adult while alone. | You may say hello, but never stop, go or take anything | You must stop and answer every question put to you | You must accompany the adult if they request it | You must accept any gift offered to remain polite
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the respect owed to a school caretaker? | It is the same as that owed to the head teacher | It is less than that owed to any teacher | It is owed solely if the caretaker is older | It is owed only in the presence of parents
